  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ナギイカダ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8A%E3%82%AE%E3%82%A4%E3%82%AB%E3%83%80",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109582177,
      "text": "ナギイカダ はスズラン亜科の常緑小低木。地中海沿岸原産で観賞用に栽培される。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ruscus aculeatus」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruscus_aculeatus",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1368593023,
      "text": "Ruscus aculeatus, known as butcher's-broom, is a low evergreen dioecious Eurasian shrub, with flat shoots known as cladodes that give the appearance of stiff, spine-tipped leaves. Small greenish flowers appear in spring, and are borne singly in the centre of the cladodes. The female flowers are followed by a red berry, and the seeds are bird-distributed, but the plant also spreads vegetatively by means of rhizomes. It is native to Eurasia and some northern parts of Africa.\nRuscus aculeatus occurs in woodlands and hedgerows, where it is tolerant of deep shade, and also on coastal cliffs. Likely due to its attractive winter/spring color, Ruscus aculeatus has become a fairly common landscape plant. \nIt is also widely planted in gardens, and has spread as a garden escapee in many areas outside its native range. The plant grows well in zones 7 to 9 on the USDA hardiness zone map."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
